    "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\nPreviously, lsp may send malformed edit requests on buffers with `setl binary noeol`: in particular, the edit request may try to delete the newline in the old buffer.\r\n\r\nTo be concrete, consider the old buffer\r\n\r\n```\r\nhello{\\n}\r\nworld{\\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhen the user deletes the second line, nvim sends \"delete line 2 column 0 → line 3 column 0\". When `eol` is set, it's all good.\r\n\r\nHowever, when eol is not set, the buffer looks like\r\n\r\n```\r\nhello{\\n}\r\nworld\r\n```\r\n\r\nbut nvim still sends \"delete line 2 column 0 → line 3 column 0\", but there's no line 3 column 0.\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nThis rewrites the logic entirely to avoid such issue: the function still assumes that `eol` is set, but tries hard to avoid emitting change region that includes the final `eol` at the end of the file. This way, the change region would work regardless of the `eol` setting.\r\n\r\n(Most of the new code is written by hand, most of the new tests are written with LLM assistance. Sorry for the large diff, but on the positive side, this new algorithm should be much simpler than the old one, at least counting from the number of non-comment lines. I did try to leave comments to explain the main idea, which is actually quite simple: we generate a valid but too large line from the input, then try to shrink it when feasible.)\r\n\r\nWhen the value of the `eol` setting changes, we resend the whole buffer: this is slightly wasteful, but we expect this event to be rare.\r\n\r\nWe don't listen for changes in the `eol` option; this may be left as future extension. Instead, a change to the `eol` option is detected on the next change to the buffer content.\r\n\r\nThe modification to the tests seem mostly harmless: I checked one of them, and it's just because of the difference between the old and the new algorithm.",
